ndard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}|{X}_{i}|;\n$$ $min({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{13},{X}_{15})$ only." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186416924 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}|{X}_{i}|$ is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only.\n\n- Expression $min({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{13},{X}_{15})$ is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15},\\sigma )$ is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}|{X}_{i}|$; $min({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{13},{X}_{15})$ only." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186416928 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expressions $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$ and $\\text{median}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$ are statistics. They are functions of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ only.\n\n- Expression $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{\\left(\\frac{{X}_{i}-\\sigma }{\\mu }\\right)}^{2}\n$$ is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu $ and the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$; $\\text{median}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$ only." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186416928 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expressions $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$ and $\\text{median}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$ are statistics. They are functions of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ only.\n\n- Expression ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{\\left(\\frac{{X}_{i}-\\sigma }{\\mu }\\right)}^{2}$ is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu $ and the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$; $\\text{median}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$ only." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186416929 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}\n$$ is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expression $\\text{mode}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6})$ is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ only.\n\n- Expression $\\frac{1}{3}({X}_{2}+{X}_{4}+{X}_{6})$ is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{mode}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}