ial equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1c/1c4133695eeb868f563f80021755fea75bef622e1ad46cbbf6daff1c953cb075.png)" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186420019 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-t}\\begi