in{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186431011 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =4.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186431011 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =4.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186431048 1074 "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by t