\frac{1}{5}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$ only." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186435740 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is also *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(4,2).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(4,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\\\\\,0<y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186435740 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is also *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(4,2).$ On the other hand, it